 "answer": {
  "answer": "Only two of the five cases were confirmed, so the goal was not met. On 096988dd the policy failed in every run with the car on time, the car at 1.25x speed and the ego at 1.2x (S5). On 19f339ba it failed in every run with the same car settings and the ego at 1.4x (S29). In both cases the 90% range lies above 0.5, and none of the failures are flagged as possible simulator artifacts. On both scenes the ego's own hand-off speed is what tipped the outcome. With the car settings unchanged and the ego slower, no run failed (S4; S27, S28). Even so, the crash was not with the retimed car. The policy drifted left out of its lane and hit a car passing beside it, which is the policy's fault. No other scene produced a single failure, and most settings were run only once or twice, so their passes are hints rather than rates.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case 1, scene 096988dd: car on time at 1.25x, ego at 1.2x. Every run failed, and the range lies above 0.5. With the ego at its recorded speed and the same car settings, no run failed. So the faster hand-off is what triggers it, though that comparison rests on only a couple of runs.",
